FINDING (pre-existing, master-inherited, OUT OF SCOPE for TC1 -- reported
for backlog, not grounds for FAIL): a single #theme-toggle click collapses
#chart-trends's rendered height from its correct filtered value (e.g. 576px
or 1678px) to 300px and it STAYS collapsed (polled 10x over 1000ms, no
recovery) on BOTH the branch (:8243) and a clean master a71e8b9 baseline
(:8253, built via `git archive master`, confirmed byte-identical mechanism).
Root cause (by code reading): charts.js:615-633's GENERIC
`window.addEventListener('themechange', ...)` handler (untouched by TC1,
pre-existing) re-renders EVERY `[id^="chart-"]` element via
`Plotly.relayout(el.id, themedLayoutUpdate(...))` without ever re-asserting
`layout.height`; combined with `.chart-container { min-height: 300px }`
(web/static/css/styles.css:85, also untouched/pre-existing), Plotly's
relayout falls back to the CSS min-height. This is a SEPARATE themechange
listener from insights.js's own (TC1-owned) `chart.data`-only restyle
handler at insights.js:446-451, which does NOT touch height and is not the
cause. Confirmed via master-baseline comparison this predates TC1 entirely
(present on `git archive master`, not introduced by attempt 1 or attempt
2's delta) and is not covered by TC.3's acceptance criteria (which specify
height only for cap-toggle/load, not theme-toggle) -- so it is not a TC1
defect under the ruling that pre-existing same-family defects outside the
task's written scope are backlog findings, not FAIL grounds (constitution
"Disputes in practice" / rule 5 of this brief). Row SET/ORDER is unaffected
by this bug (confirmed above: category set and order stay correct through
every toggle in the matrix); only the chart's visual height shrinks.

No counterexample found to "the chart is a view of the table -- same rows,
same order, same figures, current-theme markers" across the enumerated
theme x interaction states, both orders, both ranges, the mutation probe,
and the edge cases. Attempt 2's delta (render-time marker application)
demonstrably fixes ruling TC-2026-09-08b's stale-theme defect without
introducing any category-set, order, or value regression.
